Stage 14: core-ffi for every shape, the PULL decode family on a managed host,
          and the other string form. Plus a methodological correction that
          limits how far any figure in this slice travels.

commit:    0225bdea (+ this slice)
core:      ffi/poc/codec/target/release/libak_core.so, default features, the ONE
           core (R0). A second --features count build produced the crossing
           table and was swapped back out.
binding:   generated, gen/abi_ir.py -> gen/rs_probe.py (the layout probe),
           gen/cs_abi.py (the managed declaration) and gen/cs_core.py (the
           binding). One derivation, three emitters, seven roots.
R13:       1.8 ns forward, 2.1 ns forward-plus-reverse on this container.

################ what changed, and why it is one change ################

M1 and M2 each had a hand-shaped emitter, and the second cost a vtable slot
invented by analogy with a sibling. Five more written the same way would have
been five more chances at it. So the ABI declaration, the layout probe and the
host binding are now derived from one module that follows the Rust generator's
own rules, and the seven roots differ only in what that derivation returns.

The surface went from 20 structs and 5 vtables, hand-listed, to **42 structs and
28 vtables**, all verified against the Rust build's own offsets.

Two rules had to be exactly right and neither was guessable from the sibling
cases. A message that only ever appears as an INLINED child gets no vtable at
all, so `TaskOptions` is reached through `TaskDetailed`'s. And `unk_<slot>`
exists only where a run's elements are MESSAGES: a run of strings or of packed
scalars has nowhere to carry an unknown field, which is why `ak_dvt_MetricsBatch`
has seven slots and not twelve. Both were caught by the slot-count assert.

