R5: the host's tally and the core's counters are not comparable here -- this core was not built with --features count.

Reading the crossing columns. THEY ARE THE SAME QUANTITY as the core's own,
and this slice said otherwise once. The only thing the core counts that a
host tally cannot see is a TRANSCODER call, and both string forms this
binding offers are pointers into the core, so it invokes none of its own.
The gap once reported against the rust slice was a CHUNK SIZE: set
AK_CHUNK=150 and the columns match to the digit. See JOURNAL.md 19.

What the counts mean, and the whole reason the shapes differ:
  THE PULL COLUMN IS THE POINT OF THE LAST TWO. `ak_parse_*` makes no
  reverse call at all: it appends a record per deposit to a buffer in the
  host-owned decode context, and the host replays it afterwards. So pull's
  reverse count is ZERO on every shape, where push's is 2 to 3,501, and the
  two forward calls are ak_parse_* and ak_bdr_ptr. design/ABI-v1.md decision
  2 says four of five slices have only measured push; this is the managed
  pull arm it asks for, gated against push on the same bytes and the same
  comparer.

  A LEAF element batches (ABI v1 section 6), so the run crosses once
  whatever its length and the count is CONSTANT in the element count.
  A non-leaf cannot: section 7.2 refuses it, and the count becomes
  LINEAR -- a reverse call per loop slot per element on encode, and
  new+apply per element plus a run per inner field on decode.

0 failure(s)

WHAT THE GENERAL EMITTER FOUND THAT THE PER-SHAPE ONES COULD NOT.

**M3's explicit-presence STRING was silently wrong.** The kind dispatch ran
before the presence check, so an `optional string` took the implicit path: no
presence bit set, and a present-but-empty string reported identically to absent.
P3.1 encoded 10,746 bytes against 12,097. That is precisely the case
design/SHAPES.md says M3 exists to test, and neither hand-written emitter ever
reached it because neither covered a message with explicit presence. The fix
needs a staging form that sets `tc` at length zero, because `enc_blob` returns
without writing when `tc` is null and that is how an absent field is spelled.

**AND THE M1 DECODE CROSSING COUNT PUBLISHED IN STAGE 8 IS WRONG.** It said
"1 forward, 2 reverse, CONSTANT in the element count". It is not. The codec
flushes a decode run when its element arena fills -- ABI v1 7.3, a BYTE budget
divided by the group size -- so `add_results` is called `ceil(n / arena) + 1`
times and the host cannot know the arena. P1.2 is **5** reverse calls and P1.3
is **3**, not 2. Encode genuinely is constant in the element count; decode never
was, on any shape.

The R5 check caught it the moment the general gate ran a counting core over
every payload, because the host's tally was PREDICTED from the graph rather than
counted. It is now counted: every callback increments where the crossing
happens, which makes the host's number correct by construction instead of
correct by argument. R5 passes on all sixteen rows.

################ the PULL family, which is what decision 2 asked for ###########

design/ABI-v1.md decision 2: "**A pull arm on a managed host is therefore the
measurement that settles this decision in practice**, and nobody has built one."

`ak_parse_*` makes no reverse call at all. It appends a record per deposit to a
buffer in the host-owned decode context, and the host replays that buffer
afterwards. A drained buffer is a log of the reverse calls push would have made,
in the order push would have made them, so the replay is emitted from the same
slot table the push vtable is and the two families share their per-slot code.

**Gated against push on the same bytes, the same round trip and the same
generated comparer, on all sixteen payloads. Pull's reverse count is ZERO on
every shape**, against push's 2 to 3,501, and both forward calls are
`ak_parse_*` and `ak_bdr_ptr`.

And it is faster, everywhere. Ratios computed inside each process (R4):

