################ what this says ################

**1. PULL IS FASTER THAN PUSH ON EVERY SHAPE, and that is decision 2's answer
from a managed host.** pull/push is 0.69 to 0.97 with a median near 0.91, and
the one row above 1.0 is P5.4, a 4 MB bulk payload whose spread is 0.69 to 1.40
and which is saying nothing. Three to thirty-one percent, for removing the
upcalls entirely.

**2. And it moves the sign of the comparison this slice exists for.** Against
the no-boundary managed control, on the real schema's own shapes:

| payload | push / managed | pull / managed |
|---|---|---|
| P1.2 | 0.978 | **0.870** |
| P2.2 | 1.142 | **1.040** |
| P3.1 | 1.008 | **0.978** |
| P4.1 | 1.056 | **0.968** |
| P6.1 | 1.007 | **0.955** |

Push straddles 1.0 and mostly sits above it; pull mostly sits below. The
composed arm decoding a real message beats the pure C# codec **when it uses the
pull family and not otherwise**, and the margin is small either way. What the
java slice reports as a decode regression of 7.004 upcalls per element is a
statement about push, and this is the first managed measurement of the family
that does not make them.

**3. The two string forms are indistinguishable, which closes a named gap with a
null result.** `ak_tc_utf16` reads the host's own UTF-16 and `ak_tc_bytes` takes
UTF-8 the host staged; **both are pointers INTO the core, so neither costs a
crossing**, and utf16/staged is 0.96 to 1.04 on every payload. Which side does
the transcode does not matter on .NET.

That also retires a standing prediction of this slice, which was wrong about the
mechanism and not only the magnitude: stage 8 said a host transcoder would be
"one reverse crossing per string, 5,000 for P1.2, 37 to 60 us". There is no
host transcoder in that sense. The alternative form is a pointer into the core.

A true zero-copy form -- hand the core a pointer into the managed heap -- is
NOT measured, and is named rather than assumed equivalent: on .NET it needs a
pinned `GCHandle` per string, 5,000 of them for P1.2, which is a different and
probably worse trade.

**4. The group fill is 28 to 71 percent of the core-ffi encode, on all seven
shapes.** Stage 11 measured 40 to 57 on two; the wider range comes from the
shapes it could not reach. M6 is the low end at 0.278, because a packed scalar
run is a `memcpy` into the run array and no staging at all; P1.3, the absent
path, is the high end at 0.705. ABI v1 decision 9's sparse fill is still the
largest available improvement to the C# encode column, and it is now priced on
every shape rather than on two.

**5. A METHODOLOGICAL CORRECTION THAT LIMITS EVERY FIGURE IN THIS SLICE.**
Comparing this sitting with stage 11's, two arms that NEITHER change touched
moved:

    P1.2 managed-parse / gp-parse-seq   0.725 -> 0.711
    P2.2 managed-parse / gp-parse-seq   0.800 -> 0.729
    P1.2 managed / gp-marshaller        0.435 -> 0.419
    P2.2 managed / gp-marshaller        0.292 -> 0.309

Up to nine percent, on a ratio between two arms whose code is identical, taken
in separate sittings on the same container. So **a within-process ratio is sound
and a cross-SITTING comparison of two within-process ratios is not**, to better
than about ten percent. core-ffi/managed on P1.2 decode reads 0.978 here against
stage 8's 0.899 and stage 11's 0.863, and that spread is the container, not the
binding: the control moved as far in the same window.

Every same-sitting comparison above stands -- pull against push, utf16 against
staged, fill against whole -- because each is computed inside one process against
an arm that ran in the same rounds. Nothing that compares a number here with a
number from an earlier stage should be read past its first digit.

################ what is NOT measured here ################

- **An RPC arm.** The core's `rpc` feature is off in the object this stage
  loaded, and the arm design/SHAPES.md now specifies is end to end over a UDS
  against the host's real gRPC stack. This slice has the marshaller arm only.
- **A sparse-fill arm**, which paragraph 4 says is the largest remaining
  improvement. The ABI does not offer one yet; what is priced is the cost of not
  having it.
- **The zero-copy UTF-16 form**, per paragraph 3.
- **A validating-decode arm** (ABI v1 decision 3). `harness utf8` establishes
  that neither this codec nor `Google.Protobuf` validates, so the comparison is
  like for like, and the 31 open corpus `T-dec-*` vectors are what a validating
  arm would close.
- **Arms b and c for core-ffi.** net48 has no `LibraryImport` and no
  `UnmanagedCallersOnly`, so the binding does not exist on the floor runtime and
  is excluded from arm c explicitly.
- **The pull family's memory.** The record buffer is proportional to the decoded
  payload and lives in the decode context; `ak_bdr_footprint` reports it and this
  stage does not read it. Pull trades a buffer for the upcalls and only the time
  half of that trade is measured here.

